	if (ret) {
		drm_err(&xe->drm, "xe-nvm aux add failed %d\n", ret);
		auxiliary_device_uninit(aux_dev);
-		goto err;
	}
...
-err:
-	kfree(nvm);
-	xe->nvm = NULL;
-	return ret;
</text>
Reason:
After a successful `auxiliary_device_init()`, the release callback `xe_nvm_release_dev()` is set, so `auxiliary_device_uninit()` will eventually free the `nvm` memory. The original error path used a common `err` label that unconditionally called `kfree(nvm)` after `auxiliary_device_add()` failed, causing a double-free because `auxiliary_device_uninit()` had already been called (which triggers the release callback).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
	ret = auxiliary_device_init(aux_dev);
	if (ret) {
		drm_err(&xe->drm, "xe-nvm aux init failed %d\n", ret);
+		kfree(nvm);
+		xe->nvm = NULL;
+		return ret;
	}

	ret = auxiliary_device_add(aux_dev);
	if (ret) {
		drm_err(&xe->drm, "xe-nvm aux add failed %d\n", ret);
		auxiliary_device_uninit(aux_dev);
+		xe->nvm = NULL;
+		return ret;
	}
</text>
Reason:
The fix removes the shared `err` label and handles each failure separately. When `auxiliary_device_init()` fails, the `release` callback is not set, so `kfree(nvm)` is safe and must be done manually. When `auxiliary_device_add()` fails, `auxiliary_device_uninit()` triggers the release callback (which frees `nvm`), so no manual `kfree` is performed – only `xe->nvm` is set to `NULL` to avoid use‑after‑free. This eliminates the double‑free.

